  'mise_en_place': '''
TECHNIQUE: Mise en Place (Prep Organization)
RATIOS: Read the full recipe/dish once, top to bottom, BEFORE cutting anything — prevents most mid-cook scrambling. Divide the workspace into three zones: raw prep (cutting board + trim scraps), cooked/ready components (small bowls/ramekins), active cooking (stove/oven adjacent) — never let these overlap. Identify the longest-cooking component first and sequence every other prep task backward from the target serving time. Ideal tool: 4–6 small prep bowls/ramekins minimum; sheet trays for organizing larger batches. No prep bowls? Small cups, jars, or physically separated sections of a large cutting board work.
WORKFLOW: Prep everything that doesn't need last-minute freshness FIRST (aromatics, dense veg, portioned proteins) — save delicate, fast-oxidizing items (herbs, avocado, anything squeezed with citrus) for immediately before use. Use "dead time" (something simmering/resting/in the oven) to prep the next component. Clean as you go — specifically clearing boards and washing knives between raw-protein prep and anything else, which is a direct food-safety control point, not just tidiness.
READINESS CHECKPOINTS: Every component should be visible and accounted for before the first pan goes on heat. Components grouped in the order they'll be used, matching your physical layout to your cooking sequence. Aim to have mise complete with a 5–10 min buffer before active cooking starts — absorbs inevitable small surprises.
PITFALLS: (1) Starting to cook before prep is finished (e.g. burnt garlic while still dicing) — fix: pull the pan off heat entirely to catch up, rather than trying to cook and prep simultaneously under pressure. Note: starting to sweat an onion while prepping something else that goes in later is a smart move IF you know what you're doing and aren't juggling too much. (2) No plan for scraps/trim → clutter, contamination risk — fix: keep a trim bowl/compost container within arm's reach, clear it the moment the board gets crowded. (3) Underestimating prep time → rushed, sloppy work — fix: pad estimated prep time by 25–50%, especially for unfamiliar recipes or knife work you're not yet fast at.
INGREDIENT FLEX: Dense proteins can be portioned/seasoned ahead (dry brining), stored covered and refrigerated. Delicate proteins should be prepped LAST, immediately before cooking — they degrade in texture and food-safety quality fastest. Dense/root veg can be prepped furthest in advance — hold up well, minimal oxidation risk. Leafy/watery veg should be prepped close to cook time; if prepped ahead, store dry and well-wrapped to slow wilting/moisture loss. A divided tray or large sectioned plate can substitute for multiple small bowls when space is limited.
''',

  'food_storage': '''
TECHNIQUE: Food Storage Fundamentals
RATIOS: Refrigerator at or below 4°C/40°F. Freezer at -18°C/0°F or below for long-term stability. Cooling large batches (soups, stocks, stews): 60°C/140°F to 21°C/70°F within 2 hours, then 21°C/70°F to 4°C/40°F within an additional 4 hours — a real food-safety rule, not a suggestion. Shallow containers (max 5–7cm/2–3in depth) cool hot food far faster than a deep pot at the center. Ideal tool: shallow wide containers for cooling; airtight or vacuum-sealed for longer freezer storage (minimizes freezer burn via oxygen exposure). An ice bath under a pot of hot liquid dramatically speeds initial cooling before it even goes in the fridge.
STORAGE CUES: "Danger zone" is 4–60°C/40–140°F — the range where bacteria multiply fastest; the entire storage discipline exists to minimize time spent here. Date everything going into storage — "first in, first out" only works if you can see what's oldest. Raw proteins stored on the LOWEST fridge shelf, below ready-to-eat and produce — prevents drips/leaks from contaminating anything below, and that shelf also runs coldest.
QUALITY & SAFETY CHECKPOINTS: No off-color, no visible mold, no sliminess on proteins — a first-pass visual check, but don't rely on visuals alone. A sour, ammonia-like, or "off" smell is a hard stop — when in doubt, throw it out, no exceptions for food safety calls. General shelf life (refrigerated, cooked): most cooked leftovers good 3–4 days; raw proteins generally used/frozen within 1–2 days (poultry/fish) to 3–5 days (beef/pork) of purchase. Freezer: most proteins hold quality 3–6 months (longer for beef, shorter for fish) — quality (not safety) degrades gradually beyond that from freezer burn.
PITFALLS: (1) Cooling large batches in a deep pot directly in the fridge → lingers in the danger zone too long — fix: transfer to shallow containers immediately, or use an ice bath first. (2) Raw proteins stored above ready-to-eat food → cross-contamination risk from drips — fix: reorganize immediately, proteins on the bottom shelf, always. (3) No labels/dates → food gets forgotten, wasted, or worse, eaten past safety — fix: institute a simple system (masking tape + marker is enough) — a zero-waste discipline as much as a safety one.
INGREDIENT FLEX: Dense proteins freeze well — portion before freezing so you're not thawing more than needed. Delicate proteins (fish/shellfish) have the shortest safe fridge window (often 1–2 days) — buy closer to cook day, or freeze immediately. Dense/root veg is the longest-storing category by far — cool, dark, ventilated storage often suits potatoes/onions better than the fridge. Leafy/watery veg has the shortest produce shelf life — store dry, loosely wrapped, use within a few days. Reused glass jars work as well as purpose-bought containers for most storage needs, and support the zero-waste ethos directly.
''',

  'cross_contamination': '''
TECHNIQUE: Cross-Contamination Prevention
RATIOS: Minimum two cutting boards in active rotation — one dedicated to raw proteins, one for produce/ready-to-eat (color-coded boards make this foolproof). Handwashing: minimum 20 seconds with soap, specifically before AND after handling raw proteins — a hard non-negotiable checkpoint. Ideal tool: color-coded cutting boards (commonly red for raw meat, green for produce) and separate utensils/tongs for raw vs. cooked proteins. A single board can work if thoroughly washed with hot soapy water between raw-protein and produce use, but dedicated boards remove the risk of forgetting that step under time pressure.
WORKFLOW: Always prep produce/ready-to-eat items BEFORE raw proteins when sharing tools/boards, never after — minimizes the contamination window even if you forget to wash between steps. Any spoon, tongs, or plate that touched raw protein must not touch cooked/finished food without being washed first ("the tongs that put it on the grill can't take it off" unless washed between).
SAFETY CHECKPOINTS: Distinct, dedicated tools/boards clearly in use and not swapped mid-task. Wash hands until you can no longer feel any residue/oil film from raw protein handling. Treat any pause in raw-protein handling (even a short one) as a trigger to wash hands before touching anything else — phones, drawer handles, seasoning containers included.
PITFALLS: (1) Using the same plate for raw and cooked protein (classic grilling mistake) — fix: do not serve as-is, fully re-cook or discard the contaminated portion, there's no "wipe it off" fix. (2) Marinade used on raw protein then reused as a finishing sauce without boiling — fix: bring any reused marinade to a full boil for at least 1 minute first to neutralize bacterial contamination. (3) Forgetting to wash hands between raw protein and other ingredients/tools — fix: wash the moment you notice, treat it as habit-building, not just a one-off fix.
INGREDIENT FLEX: Applies universally across all ingredient categories — the discipline doesn't change based on what's being prepped, only the specific risk level shifts (raw poultry generally carries the highest cross-contamination concern of common proteins). Disposable cutting sheets are a lightweight alternative to a second board for small households or tight kitchens, discarded after raw-protein use.
''',

