  'knife_grip_mechanics': '''
TECHNIQUE: Knife Grip & Body Mechanics
RATIOS: Pinch grip — thumb and index finger pinching the blade itself just above the bolster, remaining three fingers wrapped around the handle. Non-negotiable; a full-handle "hammer grip" sacrifices control. Guide hand: "claw grip" — fingertips curled under, knuckles forward and vertical, acting as a flat guide wall for the blade; thumb tucked behind the other fingers. The blade rides against the knuckle, never past it. Damp paper towel or non-slip mat under the board — zero board movement is the target. Ideal tool: 20cm/8in chef's knife as the primary workhorse (covers ~80% of prep); paring knife (9–10cm) for small precision work; serrated knife for bread/delicate skins. A santoku is a reasonable chef's-knife substitute for smaller hands or a rocking-motion preference.
BLADE & BOARD CUES: The blade should glide through a tomato skin under its own weight, no sawing/pressing required — resistance means it's time to hone or sharpen. Board should sit dead still under moderate pressure — any sliding is a safety fail, fix it before continuing. Proper cutting motion is a forward rocking glide (tip stays largely in contact with the board), not an up-and-down chopping hack.
CUT QUALITY CHECKPOINTS: Uniform cut sizes across a batch — affects downstream cook-time evenness more than most home cooks realize. Guide-hand knuckles should feel the blade lightly brushing with every cut — that contact is what keeps fingertips safely tucked. Always cut the flattest, most stable side of an ingredient first to create a stable base (e.g. slice a small flat off a round onion or potato before cutting further).
PITFALLS: (1) Curled fingers not tucked (claw grip abandoned) → nicked fingertips — fix: stop immediately, reset guide hand into proper claw position before continuing. (2) Dull knife, forcing/sawing through food → slips and injuries — fix: hone with a honing steel before every session; a dull knife is more dangerous than a sharp one because it needs more force and less control. (3) Unstable board (wobbling/sliding) → loss of control mid-cut — fix: stop, place a damp towel or non-slip mat underneath immediately.
INGREDIENT FLEX: Dense proteins use a boning/fillet knife for breaking down whole cuts (flexible blade follows bone/joint lines); chef's knife handles slicing cooked/portioned dense proteins. Delicate proteins: a flexible fillet knife for fish; a very sharp chef's knife for clean egg/tofu cuts (dull blades crush tofu instead of slicing). Dense/root veg: chef's knife with real downward pressure and a stable rocking motion; a mandoline (with guard, always) for uniform thin slices at volume. Leafy/watery veg: chef's knife with a light touch — bruise/oxidize faster under a dull or aggressive blade.
''',

  'core_knife_cuts': '''
TECHNIQUE: Core Knife Cuts
CUT SPECS: Julienne 3mm x 3mm x 5cm (matchstick) — the building block for brunoise, and a classic garnish/quick-cook cut. Brunoise: julienne turned 90° and diced to a fine 3mm cube, used for aromatics needing to "melt" into a dish. Small dice: 6mm cube. Medium dice: 1.25cm cube. Large dice: 2cm cube — the standard escalating dice family, chosen by cook time and dish style. Chiffonade: thin ribbon cuts (1–3mm wide) of leafy herbs/greens, made by stacking, rolling tightly, then slicing across the roll. Rondelle/bias cuts: straight or angled coin-shaped slices of cylindrical vegetables (carrots, cucumbers), thickness typically 3–6mm. Ideal tool: chef's knife for all of the above; a ruler genuinely helps calibrate the eye during the first few practice sessions.
BLADE & BOARD CUES: Line up finished cuts against each other — visible size variance beyond ~1mm across a batch means slow down and tighten up. Brunoise/julienne work best with a full, controlled rocking stroke using the whole blade length, not just the tip. Keep a "work zone" (cutting) separate from a "holding zone" (finished cuts pushed to the side).
CUT QUALITY CHECKPOINTS: Clean, sharp edges on every piece — a properly sharp knife produces crisp corners, not crushed or torn edges. Pieces should stack and sit flat/uniform when piled — a strong sign of consistent sizing. Cut aromatics for a long braise/stock larger (rough chop, since they cook down); cut aromatics for a quick sauté/garnish smaller (fine dice/brunoise) since they need to cook through fast.
PITFALLS: (1) Inconsistent sizing → uneven cook times — fix: for the current batch, sort pieces by size and stagger their addition to the pan (larger first). (2) Crushed/bruised cuts (especially herbs, tomatoes) from a dull blade — fix: re-sharpen/hone before continuing; a bruised cut also oxidizes and browns faster. (3) Rushing a fine dice and losing consistency — fix: slow the tempo down, prioritize accuracy over speed — speed comes with repetition, it can't be forced early on.
INGREDIENT FLEX: Dense proteins are always cut against the grain for tenderness — direction relative to muscle fiber matters more than the size spec. Delicate proteins need minimal handling, a very sharp blade, single clean strokes rather than sawing. Dense/root veg: stable base cut first (flatten one side), then proceed to the target size — most home cooks skip this and compromise safety. Leafy/watery veg: chiffonade for herbs/leafy greens; larger dice for watery vegetables (zucchini, mushrooms) since they shrink significantly during cooking. Kitchen shears are a legitimate substitute for chiffonade-style herb cuts if knife confidence is still developing.
''',

  'knife_safety_protocol': '''
TECHNIQUE: Knife Safety Protocol
SAFETY SPECS: Store knives blade-down in a block, on a magnetic strip, or in individual sheaths — never loose in a drawer with other utensils. Carry with blade pointed down, held flush against the leg, calling out "behind" or "corner" when passing others. Hand-wash immediately after use, blade facing away from your hand, never left submerged in a sink where it can't be seen. Ideal tool: a stable, appropriately-sized cutting board (large enough food doesn't hang off the edge) is itself a core safety tool.
BLADE & BOARD CUES: Before the first cut of any session, confirm: board is stable, workspace is clear of clutter, guide hand is in claw position. If grip or focus starts slipping (common after repetitive cutting), that's the signal to pause, not push through — most kitchen cuts happen in the final few reps of a task, not the first.
SAFETY CHECKPOINTS: Guide hand knuckles ahead of fingertips at all times, blade never crosses that knuckle line. A comfortable, un-strained grip — white-knuckling the handle is a sign of poor grip mechanics, not focus. Knife goes down flat on the board any time it's not actively in hand — never left balanced on its edge or propped against something.
PITFALLS: (1) Reaching into a sink to "feel" for a submerged knife → serious cut risk — fix: never submerge knives; if it happens, always visually locate and remove it blade-first with full attention. (2) Cutting toward the guide hand instead of away/parallel — fix: stop, reposition guide hand into proper claw grip before any further cuts. (3) Using a dull knife "because sharpening takes too long" — fix: this isn't a mid-task fix, build a standing habit of honing before every session and professional sharpening every few months.
INGREDIENT FLEX: Applies universally across all ingredient categories — safety protocol doesn't change based on what's being cut, only blade choice and cut size do. A cut-resistant glove is a reasonable aid for beginners or particularly awkward ingredients (whole squash, slippery proteins), without replacing proper technique.
''',

