  'general_tips': '''
GENERAL CHEF TIPS & TRICKS (Kitchen Intuition Bank)

SEASONING & TASTING DISCIPLINE: Taste constantly, with intent — raw ingredients before cooking, at each major stage, and again right before plating. A dish seasoned only once, at the end, is a dish seasoned by guesswork. Salt in layers, not in one dump — a little at the start (aromatics), a little during (protein/veg as it cooks), a final check at the end. The mental "flavor equation" is salt, fat, acid, heat (spice), sweetness — if a dish tastes flat but you can't pinpoint why, it's almost always missing one of these five, with acid and salt the two most commonly under-used by home cooks. When a dish tastes flat, reach for acid before reaching for more salt — a squeeze of lemon or splash of vinegar often "wakes up" a dish that would otherwise get over-salted trying to fix it. Underseasoning while cooking is easy to fix; overseasoning is not — season conservatively in stages, especially with ingredients that concentrate (reductions, long braises).

HEAT & PAN INTUITION: A cold pan is the #1 cause of a bad sear, stir-fry, or sauté — preheat the pan itself (empty, dry) for a minute or two before oil ever goes in. Listen to the pan more than watch the clock — sizzle volume and rhythm tell you far more about real-time pan temperature than a timer; a quieting sizzle usually means moisture is cooking off and food is progressing toward done. If in doubt, don't crowd it — across nearly every technique in this curriculum, overcrowding is the single most common root cause of disappointing results. Let food release itself from the pan — if something's sticking hard when you try to flip it, it almost always means it isn't ready yet, not that the pan or oil failed. Preheat ovens fully, always — starting food in an under-temp oven throws off browning and timing for the entire cook.

ZERO-WASTE & INGREDIENT MAXIMIZATION (Chef Harris's signature angle): Vegetable scraps (onion skins, carrot tops, celery ends, herb stems) are stock ingredients, not trash — keep a freezer bag going, and once full it's a free stock batch. Wilting vegetables are a flavor-building opportunity, not a write-off — slightly soft carrots, celery, or peppers are perfect for a sweated aromatic base or a soup, since texture matters less once cooked down anyway. Stale bread has a second life: croutons, breadcrumbs, meatballs, panzanella, or a soup thickener. Buttermilk/dairy nearing its date is a marinade, not a discard — slightly soured dairy is often MORE useful as a tenderizing marinade base than fresh. Herb stems (cilantro, parsley) carry real flavor, especially in a blended sauce, pesto, or stock — chop finely and use as leaves rather than stripping and discarding by habit. Overripe fruit is a dessert or sauce ingredient waiting to happen (compotes, quick jams, blended into a vinaigrette for natural sweetness) rather than a compost-bin default. A weekly "use it up" cook built entirely around whatever's closest to expiration is a genuine professional-kitchen habit (family/staff meal often works this way).

TEXTURE & DONENESS INTUITION: Resistance to touch is one of the most reliable, universal doneness cues across proteins — raw is soft/yielding, well-done is firm, everything else is in between; build this instinct by comparing touch to a thermometer reading until it becomes second nature. Carryover cooking is real — pull proteins slightly before target temperature, residual heat keeps cooking for several minutes, especially for larger cuts. Rest proteins before cutting, without exception for anything larger than a single portion — cutting immediately releases juices onto the board instead of letting them redistribute. A recipe's stated cook time is a guideline, not a guarantee — pan thickness, stove strength, ingredient size, and starting temperature all shift real cook time; always cook to the visual/tactile/thermometer cue, not the clock. When texture is uncertain, cut into or press a piece to check — don't guess from the outside alone, especially with unfamiliar cuts or new-to-you recipes.

EFFICIENCY & KITCHEN WORKFLOW: Clean as you go — a workflow tool, not just tidiness; a clear board and empty sink mid-cook keeps your mind and hands free for the decisions that matter. Read the full recipe before starting, every time, even ones you've made before — missing a step you should've prepped 20 minutes ago is one of the most common, avoidable kitchen stress spirals. Keep a "flavor bombs" shelf stocked — good stock, a sharp vinegar, a favorite hot sauce, good salt, an umami booster (soy, fish sauce, parmesan rind) — these fix more last-minute flavor problems than any technique adjustment can. Batch-cook aromatics/bases when already in the kitchen — a double batch of caramelized onions or a big pot of stock takes barely more active time than a single batch, and freezes well. Sharpen/hone your knife on a schedule, not "when it feels dull" — a dull knife creeps up gradually enough that most cooks don't notice until it's already a safety and quality issue.

RECOVERY & PROBLEM-SOLVING (when it's going wrong): Too salty — add a starchy, unsalted bulk element (extra rice, potato, unsalted stock/water) to dilute, or a touch of acid/sweetness to help balance perception; there's no true "removal," only dilution and balance. Too spicy — dairy (yogurt, cream, coconut milk) neutralizes capsaicin far better than water; a touch of sugar or acid also helps balance the perception of heat. Too acidic — a small amount of fat (butter, cream, oil) or sweetness rounds out sharp acidity, added gradually while tasting. Broken sauce/emulsion — pull off heat immediately, whisk in a splash of cold liquid (water, cream, or an ice cube) off-heat to try to re-establish it before adding more fat. Burnt bottom layer (rice, sauce, braise) — do NOT scrape the burnt layer into the rest of the dish; gently transfer the unburnt portion to a fresh pot/pan, leaving the scorched layer behind entirely. Overcooked/dry protein — slice thin against the grain and serve with a sauce or jus to reintroduce moisture; it won't undo the overcooking, but it meaningfully improves the eating experience.

THE FIVE UNIVERSAL CHEF INSTINCTS (quick reference): (1) Taste at every stage — seasoning is built in layers, not fixed once at the end. (2) Don't crowd the pan/oven/grill — the single most common root cause of poor browning/texture across all techniques. (3) Preheat before food goes in — cold starting surfaces cause sticking, steaming, and uneven cooking. (4) Rest proteins, trust carryover — pulling slightly early and resting locks in juiciness and hits true target doneness. (5) Scraps and near-spoiled food are ingredients — the zero-waste mindset is a flavor-building habit, not a limitation.
''',
